/P 197 0 R
/T (2.1 Existing System)
/E (2.1 Existing System)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (    In the existing survey process, most organizations rely heavily on manual methods such as paper-based questionnaires, printed forms, or basic email surveys. These traditional approaches make the survey creation process slow and inefficient, requiring significant time and resources to design, print, and distribute each survey. Moreover, physical distribution limits the reach of the survey, as participants must be present in specific locations or receive the form directly, which reduces the number of responses and overall participation.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (     Another major drawback of the current manual system is the difficulty involved in collecting and organizing responses. Once the completed forms are returned, administrators must manually enter the data into spreadsheets or documents, which is both time-consuming and prone to errors. Mistakes such as incorrect entries, missing values, and duplicate responses can affect the accuracy of the final results. Additionally, storing paper forms requires physical space and proper maintenance, increasing the chances of data loss or damage over time.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (7)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 198 0 R
/T (2.2 Proposed System)
/E (2.2 Proposed System)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (         The proposed Online Survey System provides a fully automated, web-based solution that overcomes the limitations of the manual survey process. It allows administrators to easily create surveys with multiple question types, customize layouts, and publish them instantly to a large number of participants. By digitizing the entire workflow, the system significantly reduces the time and effort required for survey creation and distribution. This ensures faster outreach and enables institutions, companies, and researchers to gather feedback from a wider audience without geographical limitations.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (     Another major improvement of the proposed system is its ability to automatically collect and store responses in a centralized database. Users can participate in surveys from any device through a simple and responsive interface, increasing convenience and participation rates. Since the responses are stored digitally, issues related to missing forms, illegible handwriting, and manual entry errors are eliminated. The secure database ensures data integrity, while built-in validation checks help maintain accuracy and consistency in the information submitted by participants.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (8)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (       The system architecture of the Online Survey System is designed using a multi-layered approach to ensure smooth communication between the user interface, business logic, and database. At the top level, the presentation layer consists of web pages developed using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, providing a clean and responsive interface for both administrators and respondents. This layer allows users to interact with the system easily, whether they are creating surveys, answering questions, or viewing results. The focus of this layer is to ensure usability, accessibility, and a seamless experience across different devices.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (     The application layer, implemented using Java, Servlets, and JSP, acts as the core processing unit of the system. It handles all major functionalities such as user authentication, survey creation, question management, response validation, and data retrieval. This layer ensures that every user request is processed securely and efficiently before being passed to the database. By separating the application logic from the user interface, the system maintains better organization, scalability, and easier maintenance.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
